\begin{frame}{What are composite materials?}
\small
Two or more materials from the first three categories (metals, polymers, ceramics)
combined on a macroscopic scale to form a useful third material.
\vspace{2mm}
\textcolor{red!75!black}{\textbf{Advantages:}} Some properties that can be improved by forming a composite material
(should be different from those of the constituents):
\begin{columns}[T]
\column{.48\textwidth}
\begin{itemize}
\item Strength \item Stiffness \item Corrosion resistance
\item Wear resistance \item Fatigue life \item Thermal conductivity
\end{itemize}
\column{.48\textwidth}
\begin{itemize}
\item Thermal insulation \item Attractiveness \item Weight
\item Acoustical insulation \item Temperature-dependent behavior
\end{itemize}
\end{columns}
\textcolor{red!75!black}{\textbf{Note:}} Not all these properties improve at the same time.
\end{frame}

